<name>España · Madrid, Barcelona, San Sebastián</name>
<description>esp.puddnsnacks.com</description>
<Folder><name>Madrid</name>
<Placemark><name>Mercado de San Miguel</name><description>A glass-and-iron 1916 market packed with tapas, oyster and vermouth stalls next to Plaza Mayor. · Plaza de San Miguel, s/n, 28005 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7090151,40.4153741,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mercado de San Antón</name><description>Three floors in Chueca: fresh market, ready-to-eat stalls and a top-floor restaurant and terrace. · Calle de Augusto Figueroa, 24, 28004 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6975833,40.4219408,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mercado de San Fernando</name><description>A local neighborhood market with craft beer, food stalls and a books-by-weight shop. · Calle de Embajadores, 41, 28012 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7036665,40.4076696,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Buñuelos de viento at La Mallorquina</name><description>A bakery from 1894 that fries its All Saints buñuelos the same day it sells them. · Puerta del Sol, 8, 28013 Madrid (also Velázquez 39 and Arapiles 1)</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7047732,40.4166278,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Huesos de santo at Antigua Pastelería del Pozo</name><description>Madrid&#x27;s oldest bakery (1830) makes filled buñuelos and marzipan huesos de santo for All Saints. · Calle del Pozo, 8, 28012 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7011705,40.416256,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>All Saints sweets at El Riojano</name><description>An 1855 confectioner with a period interior that sells buñuelos and huesos de santo for a short season. · Calle Mayor, 10, 28013 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7059265,40.4164965,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Paella or tapas cooking class at Cooking Point</name><description>Hands-on class where you cook paella or tapas with a local chef and eat what you make. · Calle de Moratín, 11, 28014 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6974863,40.412403,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Suma Flamenca festival: final week at Teatros del Canal</name><description>Top flamenco stars in a modern theater during the closing week of Madrid&#x27;s big flamenco festival. · Calle de Cea Bermúdez, 1, 28003 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7051542,40.438221,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Flamenco Real: Arcángel at Teatro Real</name><description>One of today&#x27;s great flamenco singers in an intimate ballroom inside the opera house. · Plaza de Isabel II, s/n, 28013 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.710579,40.4182199,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Flamenco show at Corral de la Morería</name><description>Madrid&#x27;s most famous tablao, open since 1956, with a dinner option. · Calle de la Morería, 17, 28005 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7141978,40.4126583,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Flamenco show at Tablao Cardamomo</name><description>A central tablao with four shows a night, so it fits around a late dinner. · Calle de Echegaray, 15, 28014 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6994334,40.4154423,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Jazz at Café Central (Ulf Wakenius Organ Trio and more)</name><description>Madrid&#x27;s classic jazz club, now in the Ateneo building, with a Swedish guitar great on the weekend. · Calle del Prado, 21, 28014 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6982321,40.4151962,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>JazzMadrid at Teatro Albéniz: Lizz Wright, Alfa Mist, Richard Bona</name><description>Three big international jazz names on three nights in a central theater, part of the JazzMadrid festival. · Calle de la Paz, 11, 28012 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7040234,40.4154216,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ibrahim Maalouf &amp; The Trumpets of Michel-Ange Vol. 2</name><description>The French-Lebanese trumpet star brings his brass-heavy jazz show to a classic Madrid club. · Paseo Bajo de la Virgen del Puerto, s/n, 28005 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7221336,40.4129548,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>London Philharmonic, Edward Gardner, Julia Fischer</name><description>A top London orchestra with star violinist Julia Fischer in Spain&#x27;s main concert hall. · Calle del Príncipe de Vergara, 146, 28002 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6776776,40.445983,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Spanish National Orchestra and Choir: Kent Nagano, Debussy and Falla&#x27;s Atlàntida</name><description>Debussy&#x27;s La Mer and a selection from Falla&#x27;s epic Atlàntida, plus a world-premiere Debussy orchestration. · Calle del Príncipe de Vergara, 146, 28002 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6776776,40.445983,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Morat in concert</name><description>The Colombian pop-folk band plays a multi-night arena run, the biggest pop show in town these dates. · Not found in a cited listing (Movistar Arena, Goya area, 28009 Madrid per OpenStreetMap)</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6717885,40.4238624,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thyssen: Mauritshuis masterpieces, Dalí and Kenny Scharf</name><description>Twenty-five Dutch masterpieces on loan from The Hague, plus a new show on Dalí and Freud. · Paseo del Prado, 8, 28014 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6949317,40.4162159,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Prado Museum and &#x27;Ricardo de Madrazo: drawings and watercolors&#x27;</name><description>Velázquez, Goya and Bosch, plus a new show of Ricardo de Madrazo&#x27;s drawings (Oct 13, 2026–Jan 17, 2027). · Paseo del Prado, s/n, 28014 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6920407,40.4137925,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Reina Sofía: Eli Cortiñas, Manolo Millares and the Guernica collection</name><description>Picasso&#x27;s Guernica plus new shows by Eli Cortiñas and Fernando Sánchez Castillo, free in the evening. · Calle de Santa Isabel, 52, 28012 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6944216,40.4080495,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Andrea Canepa&#x27;s &#x27;Fardo&#x27; at the Palacio de Cristal</name><description>A huge colorful textile wraps the glass palace in the Retiro, a free outdoor art stop. · Paseo de la República de Cuba, 4, 28009 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6820649,40.4135979,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>&#x27;La otra orilla de la vanguardia&#x27; at Fundación MAPFRE</name><description>How avant-garde artists were shaped by art made by psychiatric patients, from Prinzhorn&#x27;s 1922 book. · Paseo de Recoletos, 23, 28004 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6922714,40.4227331,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>&#x27;We Are Nature&#x27; at CaixaForum Madrid</name><description>A show on people and nature in the building with the famous vertical garden wall, steps from the Prado. · Paseo del Prado, 36, 28014 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6935679,40.4111396,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>&#x27;La transición estética. España, 1960–2020&#x27; at Fundación Juan March</name><description>A free show on how modern art reached Spain from 1960 to 2020, with Warhol, Kandinsky and Matisse. · Calle de Castelló, 77, 28006 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.6814448,40.4312743,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>MADHORROR horror culture festival (Halloween)</name><description>First edition of a giant horror fest with effects exhibits, a zombie march and guests like Robert Englund. · Avenida del Partenón, 5, 28042 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.617214,40.4663707,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Barcelona</name>
<Placemark><name>Panellets at Escribà</name><description>A famous old pastry shop and an easy place to try panellets, the almond-and-sweet-potato sweets of All Saints. · La Rambla, 83, 08002 Barcelona (second shop: Gran Via de les Corts Catalanes, 546)</description><Point><coordinates>2.1724,41.3815,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mercat de la Boqueria</name><description>Barcelona&#x27;s best-known food market, full of ham, seafood, fruit and counter bars. · La Rambla, 89, 08001 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1716,41.3818,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mercat de Santa Caterina</name><description>A calmer, local market under a famous wavy, colorful roof. · Av. Francesc Cambó, 16, 08003 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1786,41.3863,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mercat de Sant Antoni and Sunday book market</name><description>A restored iron market hall with food on weekdays and a classic secondhand book and collectibles market on Sunday mornings. · Carrer del Comte d&#x27;Urgell, 1, 08011 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1623,41.379,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Vermut and montaditos at Quimet &amp; Quimet</name><description>A tiny standing-room family bodega from 1914, famous for house vermut and stacked montaditos. · Carrer del Poeta Cabanyes, 25, 08004 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1658,41.3738,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cook &amp; Taste paella class with market tour</name><description>A hands-on class where you cook tapas, paella and dessert, then eat it with local wine. · Carrer del Paradís, 3, 08002 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1771,41.3836,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hermeto&#x27;s Mothership (Barcelona Jazz Festival)</name><description>Hermeto Pascoal&#x27;s longtime band plays an all-Hermeto tribute in a small cellar club. · Plaça Reial, 17, 08002 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1752,41.38,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gran Gala Flamenco at the Palau de la Música</name><description>Flamenco on the stage of a Modernista concert hall with a stained-glass skylight. · Carrer Palau de la Música, 4-6, 08003 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1753,41.3875,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nate Smith (Barcelona Jazz Festival)</name><description>A top modern jazz drummer mixes jazz with funk and soul in a classic old dance hall. · Carrer Nou de la Rambla, 111, 08004 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1697,41.3744,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bellini&#x27;s I Capuleti e i Montecchi at the Liceu</name><description>Bel canto Romeo and Juliet with Lisette Oropesa in Barcelona&#x27;s grand opera house. · La Rambla, 51-59, 08002 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1734,41.3807,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Branford Marsalis Quartet and Dianne Reeves (Barcelona Jazz Festival)</name><description>Two jazz greats share a bill in one of Europe&#x27;s most beautiful concert halls. · Carrer Palau de la Música, 4-6, 08003 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1753,41.3875,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sant Andreu Jazz Band All-Stars (Barcelona Jazz Festival)</name><description>Barcelona&#x27;s famous youth jazz band marks 20 years with Sinatra and Armstrong songs in a 1900s dance hall. · Carrer del Tigre, 27, Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.165,41.384,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kid Francescoli</name><description>Dreamy French electro-pop in Barcelona&#x27;s best-known indie club. · Carrer de Pamplona, 88, 08018 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.191,41.3977,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kurt Rosenwinkel: The Remedy (Barcelona Jazz Festival)</name><description>The guitarist reunites his 2006 Village Vanguard quintet to revisit a modern jazz classic. · Carrer Nou de la Rambla, 88, 08001 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1712,41.3755,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Flamenco at Tablao Cordobés</name><description>A long-running flamenco tablao with a 70-minute show close to the dancers. · La Rambla, 35, 08002 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1748,41.379,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Flamenco at Los Tarantos</name><description>A short, affordable flamenco show in a historic basement on Plaça Reial. · Plaça Reial, 17, 08002 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1753,41.3801,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Museu Picasso</name><description>The best place to see young Picasso, set in medieval palaces in El Born. · Carrer de Montcada, 15-23, 08003 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1808,41.3852,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>MNAC (Museu Nacional d&#x27;Art de Catalunya)</name><description>Romanesque church murals and Catalan Modernisme in a palace with a huge city view. · Palau Nacional, Parc de Montjuïc, 08038 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1534,41.3685,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>MACBA (Museu d&#x27;Art Contemporani de Barcelona)</name><description>Barcelona&#x27;s main contemporary art museum, in a bright white building with skaters out front. · Plaça dels Àngels, 1, 08001 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1667,41.3833,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fundació Joan Miró</name><description>Miró&#x27;s own museum on Montjuïc, now with a big Charlotte Perriand design show. · Parc de Montjuïc, 08038 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1599,41.3685,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>&#x27;The Cult of Beauty&#x27; at the CCCB</name><description>A big final-days show on how ideas of beauty have shaped us, right next to MACBA. · Carrer de Montalegre, 5, 08001 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.167,41.3837,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Casa Batlló and &#x27;Gaudí-Miró-Gomis: Deconstructed&#x27;</name><description>Gaudí&#x27;s dragon-roofed house plus a new digital show pairing Gaudí, Miró and photographer Joaquim Gomis. · Passeig de Gràcia, 43, 08007 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.165,41.3916,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>La Pedrera (Casa Milà)</name><description>Gaudí&#x27;s wavy stone apartment building with a rooftop of warrior-like chimneys. · Passeig de Gràcia, 92, 08008 Barcelona</description><Point><coordinates>2.1619,41.3954,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Park Güell</name><description>Gaudí&#x27;s hilltop park with mosaic benches, the dragon stair and wide city views. · Gràcia district (La Salut); nearest metro L3 Vallcarca</description><Point><coordinates>2.1527,41.4145,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>San Sebastián</name>
<Placemark><name>Gandarias</name><description>A busy, reliable Old Town bar that stays open every day, including Sunday and Monday. · Calle 31 de Agosto 23, 20003 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9852,43.3238,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>La Cuchara de San Telmo</name><description>Small back-lane bar behind San Telmo Museoa, famous for cooked-to-order hot pintxos. · Calle 31 de Agosto 28, 20003 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9849,43.3241,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ganbara</name><description>The classic stop for wild mushrooms, the bar&#x27;s specialty. · Calle San Jerónimo 21, 20003 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.986,43.3236,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bar Néstor</name><description>The famous potato tortilla, made only twice a day, plus aged txuleta steak. · Arrandegi Kalea (Calle Pescadería) 11, 20003 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9853,43.3234,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>La Viña</name><description>Home of the creamy burnt Basque cheesecake. · Calle 31 de Agosto 3, 20003 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9867,43.3243,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bar Bergara</name><description>The best-known pintxo bar in Gros, open every day. · General Artetxe 8, 20002 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9789,43.3218,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>La Bretxa and San Martín markets</name><description>See the fish, produce and cheese that end up on the pintxo counters. · La Bretxa: Boulevard Zumardia 3, 20003. San Martín: Calle Loyola s/n, 20006.</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9838,43.3227,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sidrería Petritegi (cider house)</name><description>Txotx season runs mid-January to late April, but Petritegi serves the full cider-house meal all year. · Petritegi Bidea, 20115 Astigarraga</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9426,43.2885,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pintxos tasting paired with Spanish wine (tour)</name><description>An easy first night: a guide picks the bars and orders for you. · Alderdi Eder / Boulevard kiosk, 20003 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9845,43.3225,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Basque cuisine cooking class</name><description>Learn to cook Basque dishes hands-on in the Old Town. · Calle 31 de Agosto 29, 20003 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.985,43.3241,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Akelarre (Pedro Subijana)</name><description>Three-star tasting menu with a sea view over the Bay of Biscay. · Padre Orcolaga 56, Igeldo, 20008 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-2.0403,43.3062,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Martín Berasategui</name><description>One of Spain&#x27;s most decorated chefs, at his flagship three-star restaurant. · Loidi 4, 20160 Lasarte-Oria</description><Point><coordinates>-2.0187,43.2701,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Arzak and Mugaritz (both closed on your dates)</name><description>Skip on this trip: both are closed, so book Akelarre or Berasategui instead. · Arzak: Avda. Alcalde José Elosegi 273, 20015 Donostia. Mugaritz: Errenteria (address not checked).</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9475,43.3196,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Day trips: Getaria (txakoli, Balenciaga) and Hondarribia (pintxos)</name><description>Getaria has txakoli vineyards, grilled fish and the Balenciaga museum; Hondarribia has a painted fishermen&#x27;s street full of pintxo bars. · Getaria tourist office: Kale Nagusia 38, 20808 Getaria</description><Point><coordinates>-2.2044,43.3036,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Rosana in concert</name><description>Spanish pop singer-songwriter in the city&#x27;s landmark glass concert hall, on arrival night. · Avenida de Zurriola 1, 20002 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9783,43.324,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Basque National Orchestra: Aita Donostia, La vie profonde de Saint François d&#x27;Assise</name><description>A local orchestra and choirs performing music by San Sebastián&#x27;s own composer-priest. · Avenida de Zurriola 1, 20002 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9783,43.324,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dabadaba club shows: okvsho, HABI + Etxekalte, The Sonic Beat Explosion</name><description>The city&#x27;s main small indie club, a good local night out. · Mundaitz Kalea 8, 20012 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9763,43.3169,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>PUTOCHINOMARICÓN live</name><description>Madrid electro-pop artist in the club space inside Tabakalera. · Plaza de las Cigarreras 1, 20012 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9786,43.317,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>37th Horror and Fantasy Film Week (final days)</name><description>A fun, rowdy local film festival with cheap tickets in historic theaters. · Teatro Principal: Kale Nagusia 3, Parte Vieja (address not verified). Victoria Eugenia: Reina Regente 8 (address not verified).</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9858,43.3232,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tabakalera and Kubo Kutxa exhibitions</name><description>The city&#x27;s contemporary culture hub, with free film-art shows and a rooftop view. · Plaza de las Cigarreras 1, 20012 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9786,43.317,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>San Telmo Museoa</name><description>Basque history and art in a 16th-century convent, with the Sert murals in the old church. · Plaza Zuloaga 1, 20003 Donostia (address not verified on a fetched page)</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9842,43.3247,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Chillida Leku</name><description>Eduardo Chillida&#x27;s huge iron and stone sculptures set in fields around an old farmhouse. · Barrio Jauregui 66, 20120 Hernani</description><Point><coordinates>-1.9927,43.2853,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Peine del Viento (Comb of the Wind)</name><description>Chillida&#x27;s three steel sculptures gripping the rocks, best when the sea is rough. · West end of Ondarreta beach, at the foot of Monte Igeldo</description><Point><coordinates>-2.005,43.3218,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cristina Enea park</name><description>A quiet 19th-century garden with peacocks and old trees, right next to Tabakalera. · Paseo Duque de Mandas 66, 20012 Donostia</description><Point><coordinates>-1.975,43.3144,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Guggenheim Bilbao (day trip)</name><description>Steve McQueen and Pipilotti Rist video shows inside Gehry&#x27;s famous building. · Bilbao (about 1 hour+ from San Sebastián; transport not checked)</description><Point><coordinates>-2.934,43.2687,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Botín and other roasts</name>
<Placemark><name>Restaurante Sobrino de Botín (Casa Botín)</name><description>Calle de Cuchilleros 17, 28005 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.707981,40.4141745,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Casa Lucio</name><description>A La Latina classic famous for its broken eggs. It also serves roast suckling pig, so you get both in one meal. · Calle Cava Baja 35, 28005 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7096742,40.4120917,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Posada de la Villa</name><description>An inn from 1642 on Cava Baja, restored in 1980 as a roasting house. It is known for wood-oven lamb. Cocido is also available if you order ahead. · Calle Cava Baja 9, 28005 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7084475,40.4130547,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Los Galayos</name><description>Right on Plaza Mayor, a short walk from Botín. The pigs come from Segovia with the guarantee label. The kitchen never closes in the afternoon, so it&#x27;s an easy backup. · Calle Botoneras 5 / Plaza Mayor 1, 28012 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7066972,40.4148825,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>El Landó</name><description>Owned by Lucio Blázquez of Casa Lucio. It has the same famous eggs in a quieter setting by the Vistillas gardens, with views of the Almudena Cathedral. · Plaza de Gabriel Miró 8, 28005 Madrid</description><Point><coordinates>-3.7146654,40.4123441,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mesón de Cándido</name><description>It sits at the foot of the Roman aqueduct. This is the house that made Segovia cochinillo famous, and it still does the plate-cutting and plate-smashing show. · Plaza del Azoguejo 5, 40001 Segovia</description><Point><coordinates>-4.1178861,40.9476582,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Restaurante José María</name><description>A favorite with locals since 1982. It raises its own piglets on its own farm. It has a lively tapas bar, and the menu marks vegetarian dishes, which helps a mixed table. · Calle Cronista Lecea 11, 40001 Segovia (next to Plaza Mayor)</description><Point><coordinates>-4.1227292,40.9505005,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Casa Duque</name><description>Opened in 1895. It calls itself Segovia&#x27;s oldest restaurant, and the fourth and fifth generations of the family run it. It sits on the walking street between the aqueduct and Plaza Mayor. · Calle Cervantes 12 (Calle Real), 40001 Segovia</description><Point><coordinates>-4.1198758,40.9477918,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>La Sagrada Família</name>
<Placemark><name>Basílica de la Sagrada Família</name><description>Carrer de Mallorca, 401, 08013 Barcelona. Individual visitors enter at the Nativity facade on Carrer de la Marina. Metro L2 and L5, Sagrada Família stop.</description><Point><coordinates>2.1744,41.4036,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Cava</name>
<Placemark><name>Codorníu</name><description>The birthplace of Cava. Modernista cellars by Josep Puig i Cadafalch, built 1895-1915, and a small train ride through the underground tunnels. · Avinguda Jaume de Codorníu, s/n, 08770 Sant Sadurní d&#x27;Anoia</description><Point><coordinates>1.79758,41.43375,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Freixenet</name><description>Easiest visit by train. Big, classic Cava house. · C/ de Joan Sala, 2, 08770 Sant Sadurní d&#x27;Anoia</description><Point><coordinates>1.79363,41.42043,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Recaredo</name><description>A founding Corpinnat winery. Several visit levels, from a 2-hour visit to private vineyard tours. · C/ Tamarit, 10, 08770 Sant Sadurní d&#x27;Anoia</description><Point><coordinates>1.7869,41.42514,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gramona</name><description>A founding Corpinnat family winery in town. · Carrer de la Indústria, 36, 08770 Sant Sadurní d&#x27;Anoia</description><Point><coordinates>1.77299,41.42698,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Raventós i Blanc</name><description>The first winery to leave the Cava DO (2012). Run by descendants of Josep Raventós. · Plaça del Roure, s/n, 08770 Sant Sadurní d&#x27;Anoia</description><Point><coordinates>1.79694,41.43414,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Juvé &amp; Camps</name><description>Big family house that just switched to Corpinnat in 2026. · Carrer de Sant Venat, 1, 08770 Sant Sadurní d&#x27;Anoia</description><Point><coordinates>1.7919,41.42455,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Torelló</name><description>Family estate with a vineyard walk and a masia (farmhouse) courtyard. Corpinnat founder. · Carretera C-243b, km 13.4 (Gelida to Sant Sadurní road), 08790 Gelida</description><Point><coordinates>1.83799,41.43265,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Llopart</name><description>Montserrat views over the vines. Corpinnat founder. · Carretera de l&#x27;Ordal a Sant Sadurní d&#x27;Anoia, Subirats (street number not found)</description><Point><coordinates>1.80354,41.40801,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Agustí Torelló Mata (Kripta)</name><description>Home of the Kripta cava; small English or Spanish visit you can request by date. · Not confirmed (OpenStreetMap places it on road BP-2151, la Riera, 08775)</description><Point><coordinates>1.7659,41.43543,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>El Xampanyet</name><description>Carrer de Montcada, 22, 08003 Barcelona (El Born)</description><Point><coordinates>2.18167,41.38451,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Can Paixano (La Xampanyeria)</name><description>Carrer de la Reina Cristina, 7, 08003 Barcelona (near Jaume I metro, L4)</description><Point><coordinates>2.18343,41.38178,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
